in here and she  ended up here and we are now just  bringing her back to where she started  we went through a living hell  you hand your child overthinking they're  going to get help and they're going to  get care  and the minute you realize that um  they're not getting that  um and more and worse than ever that  actually you can't do anything about it  but Amber's is not an isolated case just  a month before the report into Amber's  care Hunter come faced similar criticism  at the same unit over another teenager  who were calling Sally  an independent review into Sally's care  shared with Sky News found over Sally's  four-month stay she was restrained more  than 200 times it also recommended an  urgent audit into the use of restraint  we've been going back through the  reports of inspectors from the regulator  the Care Quality commission and found  repeated warnings about units run by the  huntercom group we've cross-referenced  these with the testimonies of the former  patients we've traced we have more than  20 statements verbal and written  accounts from former patients or their  relatives these were young girls at  Breaking Point with serious mental  health issues when they went into  Hospital needing specialist help  the people we've spoken to were in the  units over a 10-year period some as  recently as February and March this year  Lyra went into huntercom's hospitals  when she was just turning 14. she told  us on leaving their care I was more  mentally ill than when I was admitted  Danielle who was in the maidenhead unit  said I break down crying it affects me  every single day  Danny was in the Stafford unit for two  years for an eating disorder she told us  she was injured during restraints and  would often be left with bruising and  had her hair pulled out she shared these  pictures which she says are staff  sleeping when they should have been  monitoring at-risk patients  another teenager who wants to remain  anonymous said she was able to hide in a  corner of her room and self-harm without  staff noticing her arm bleeding  the sister of another patient said each  time she visited her sister appeared  more and more drugged up to the point at  times she could hardly communicate  Nema was groomed during her stay at  Hunter comes Whatcom Hall Hospital in  Devon which has now closed she told us  she was subjected to countless Horrors  and staff were cold and cruel she said  after leaving I had to learn to live  again  all these teenagers say they were failed  by the care they received  but for years the hanticum group has  been aware of the serious criticisms  it's faced in 2014 it was sued by  Natasha Beauchamp for failing to provide  appropriate psychiatric treatment  in 2017 the regulator the Care Quality  commission raised concerns about  Staffing observations and how to put  Lessons Learned into practice at all its  sites in the same year  seventeen-year-old Mia tetherage took  her own life at the Norwich unit as  subsequent inquest said she wasn't  properly checked  a year and a half after Mia's death a  teenager went into the group's  maidenhead unit who were calling Jane  Jane wants to remain anonymous broke me  made things worse and I don't think I'm  the only one we're not showing Jane's  face or her mother's but they've agreed  for their voices to be used Jane went  into the maidenhead unit with autism and  low-level self-harming when she was 14.  there was another visitor room  and that's the one that we were mostly  taken to it was a place Jane's mother  says she came to know well everything  about that time remains Vivid for both  of them Jane is now 18.  such a horrible place that people resort  to such  horrible things when I walked in there's  blood all over the walls and people just  literally whack their head against the  wall Jane's mother kept her diary of her  visits to the maidenhead unit I saw the  children whacking their heads I saw a  child kneeing herself in the face I saw  a young man I can only describe him as a  young man he didn't look like a child  masturbating in the middle of the  corridor it's like what you read about  asylums and work houses the clear trauma  and pain and suffering and they were  children and they were treated worse  than animals Jane's care was examined in  a subsequent independent review shared  with Sky News and concluded there was  little offered in terms of actual  treatment it described the unit as a  dark space that had an oppressive feel I  just get flashbacks  of what it looks like  like what it smelled like  just  what it like felt like and just and then  obviously the individual things that  happen whilst you're there but just the  whole thing  again and again former patients told us  the hospitals were places where they  felt they wouldn't get better  I knew the moment I arrived there it  wasn't the right place for me  those screaming there was alarms going  off there was blood on the walls it was  yeah it was pretty bad it's a year now  since Alice left maidenhead she went in  at the age of 16 she says in crisis  severely self-harming and with an eating  disorder you sprained your wrist in your  ankle yeah yeah because they weren't  holding me correctly yeah  another former patient complaining not  just about the techniques used to stop  herself harming but detailing how she  felt the environment put her at even  greater risk often a lot of other  patients were using different  self-harming methods it's staff often  didn't let didn't stop them from doing  that so they'd do it in like communal  areas and I would learn new ways to do  to self-harm I think it's awful I just  don't think that  any place like that that treated me like  that should be open I don't even know  why they're still running  when you read these testimonies what do  you make of them you know there's  references here to Blood on the walls  describing treatment that sounds  barbaric we showed our findings to Anne  Longfield the former children's  commissioner who's now chairing an  independent review of inpatient mental  health care the CQC really does need to  take this seriously and needs to visit  at speed to ensure they get an  understanding of what's going on there  Sky News can reveal NHS England has paid  nearly 190 million pounds to run  Children's Mental Health Services at a  number of huntercom sites since 2015.  the kind of eye-watering thumbs that  we're given to Providers to provide  Specialist Care clearly what what what  you're showing here is that that was  making some children much worse on every  level that's not something that's  acceptable at Active Care Group we  strive to ensure our patients residents  and clients are kept safe in December  2021 the hanticum group was taken over  by Active Care Group which told us it  has robust processes in place Dr Sylvia  Tang has been the CEO since June 2020 a  year and a half before the merger  in a statement she said at the time most  of these incidents are alleged to have  taken place the Active Care Group did  not own the facilities at which they are  purported to have happened the previous  owners retain all the legal entities and  Associated records for these facilities  prior to their acquisition the current  group is therefore unable to comment on  the services provided prior to this date  even so we are very saddened and  concerned to hear about these patient  experiences  the previous owners Ellie Investments  said it regrets the hospitals which were  independently managed failed to meet the  expected standards for high quality Care  in February this year two months after  the acquisition of the hanticum group by  Active Care Group a patient at the unit  in maidenhead now called taplo Manor  died what happened is now the subject of  an investigation by Thames Valley police  and the CQC  Active Care Group says the death of a  young person at taplo Manor in February  2022 was a tragic and sad event we have  already taken steps to make improvements  to the service and remain committed to  providing the best possible care for our  patients  the NHS is currently paying for nearly a  hundred beds at the huntercom units now  owned by Active Care Group maidenhead is  rated as requiring Improvement and  Staffordshire inadequate  an NHS spokesperson said all services  must provide safe high quality care and  deliver on the commitments in their  contracts the CQC said Active Care Group  is subject to close and ongoing  monitoring where we find issues we have  taken and continue to take decisive  action  friends come here Amber is one of a  number of former patients we spoke to  for whom their time at huntercom's  maidenhead unit contributed to a formal  diagnosis of post-traumatic stress  disorder  even when I'm out with my friends it  comes up in every conversation  even though it's so much over it's not  at all because it ruined my life  sometimes  I'll just be normally sat here and I can  look around and I'm there again I can  see the walls I can smell it I can hear  it I can feel people on me I can feel  people holding me and it's horrible it  happens a lot and it hasn't stopped  every day guaranteed that it's been  going through my head more than anything  else  which is messed up because I just want  to move on  in response to our report the Department  of Health told us the allegations of  mistreatment raised by Sky News are  deeply concerning it's now working with  NHS England and the CQC to investigate
 

